From: Yocto build fix
Subject: [PATCH] Messenger: track in-flight message reassembly per channel

Symptom: an active AndroidAuto session (video streaming + at least one
continuous audio channel running concurrently) reliably tears itself
down anywhere from ~10 seconds to a couple of minutes in, always with
every channel logging the same error simultaneously:

  [error] [VideoService] channel error: AaSdk error code: 29, native code: 0
  [error] [AudioService] channel error: AaSdk error code: 29, ...
  [info]  [App] quit.

Error code 29 is ErrorCode::MESSENGER_INTERTWINED_CHANNELS, thrown
from MessageInStream::receiveFrameHeaderHandler.

Root cause: MessageInStream reassembles a message that spans multiple
frames (FrameType::FIRST/MIDDLE/LAST) using a single scalar
Message::Pointer message_ (plus a single FrameType recentFrameType_)
shared across every channel. It assumed a channel's multi-frame
message always completes before any other channel's frame can appear
on the wire, and treated a frame header naming a different channel
while message_ was still in progress as a fatal protocol violation.

That assumption doesn't hold: the transport is one multiplexed byte
stream, and the phone is free to (and in practice does, once a large
multi-frame VIDEO message overlaps with concurrently streaming
MEDIA_AUDIO/SPEECH_AUDIO/SYSTEM_AUDIO frames) interleave frames
belonging to different channels while one of them still has a
multi-frame message in flight. That's routine multiplexing, not
corruption, but the single shared message_/recentFrameType_ fields
had no way to represent "more than one channel's message is currently
partway through reassembly", so the first legitimate interleave after
two channels are both actively streaming got misread as
MESSENGER_INTERTWINED_CHANNELS and killed the whole AndroidAutoEntity
session -- explaining both the intermittent timing (depends on when
frame timing happens to interleave) and why it reproduces on every
phone/device tested.

Fix: key the in-progress reassembly state (the partially-built Message
plus its most recent FrameType) by ChannelId in a map instead of a
pair of scalars, so each channel's multi-frame message is reassembled
independently regardless of how the sender interleaves frames from
other channels on the wire. The transport itself is unaffected -- only
one physical read is ever outstanding at a time, exactly as before;
this only fixes which channel's accumulator each completed frame's
payload gets routed into.

This is safe with respect to the shared Cryptor: EncryptionType is
per-frame, and in practice only small single-frame (BULK) control
messages (open/setup/focus/ping) are ever ENCRYPTED -- continuous
high-bandwidth VIDEO/AUDIO media frames that actually interleave are
PLAIN and never touch the cryptor, so decrypt() call ordering relative
to the single shared SSL/BIO stream is unchanged.

Upstream-Status: Pending

diff --git a/include/f1x/aasdk/Messenger/MessageInStream.hpp b/include/f1x/aasdk/Messenger/MessageInStream.hpp
index 262c3f5..89ccb64 100644
--- a/include/f1x/aasdk/Messenger/MessageInStream.hpp
+++ b/include/f1x/aasdk/Messenger/MessageInStream.hpp
@@ -18,6 +18,7 @@
 
 #pragma once
 
+#include <map>
 #include <f1x/aasdk/Transport/ITransport.hpp>
 #include <f1x/aasdk/Messenger/IMessageInStream.hpp>
 #include <f1x/aasdk/Messenger/ICryptor.hpp>
@@ -41,16 +42,31 @@ public:
 private:
     using std::enable_shared_from_this<MessageInStream>::shared_from_this;
 
+    // A single logical message may be split across multiple frames
+    // (FIRST/MIDDLE/LAST). The transport is a single multiplexed byte
+    // stream, and the sender is free to interleave frames belonging to
+    // different channels while one of them still has a multi-frame
+    // message in progress (this is routine once, e.g., a large VIDEO
+    // frame and concurrent MEDIA_AUDIO frames are both in flight) --
+    // so in-progress reassembly state has to be tracked per channel
+    // rather than as a single pair of fields, or an interleaved frame
+    // from another channel gets misread as a protocol violation
+    // (MESSENGER_INTERTWINED_CHANNELS) and tears down the whole session.
+    struct PendingMessage
+    {
+        Message::Pointer message;
+        FrameType recentFrameType;
+    };
+
     void receiveFrameHeaderHandler(const common::DataConstBuffer& buffer);
-    void receiveFrameSizeHandler(const common::DataConstBuffer& buffer);
-    void receiveFramePayloadHandler(const common::DataConstBuffer& buffer);
+    void receiveFrameSizeHandler(const common::DataConstBuffer& buffer, ChannelId channelId);
+    void receiveFramePayloadHandler(const common::DataConstBuffer& buffer, ChannelId channelId);
 
     boost::asio::io_service::strand strand_;
     transport::ITransport::Pointer transport_;
     ICryptor::Pointer cryptor_;
-    FrameType recentFrameType_;
     ReceivePromise::Pointer promise_;
-    Message::Pointer message_;
+    std::map<ChannelId, PendingMessage> pendingMessages_;
 };
 
 }
